    'Between the eighth and ninth centuries CE, many great Maya cities <select class="pte-select" data-answer="experienced"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="experiencing">experiencing</option><option value="experiences">experiences</option><option value="experience">experience</option><option value="experienced">experienced</option></select> a rapid decline in population. Prolonged droughts likely <select class="pte-select" data-answer="reduced"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="reduced">reduced</option><option value="increased">increased</option><option value="boosted">boosted</option><option value="stabilized">stabilized</option></select> agricultural production in the region, straining food supplies. Some scholars argue that warfare between rival city-states also proved <select class="pte-select" data-answer="damaging"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="damaging">damaging</option><option value="reassuring">reassuring</option><option value="beneficial">beneficial</option><option value="irrelevant">irrelevant</option></select> to political stability. Although lowland cities were abandoned, Maya culture <select class="pte-select" data-answer="persisted"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="persist">persist</option><option value="persisted">persisted</option><option value="persisting">persisting</option><option value="persists">persists</option></select> in the northern Yucatan for centuries afterward.',
