    'In the context of global public health, governments face unprecedented challenges. The most <select class="pte-select" data-answer="pressing"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="pressing">pressing</option><option value="relaxed">relaxed</option><option value="minor">minor</option><option value="solved">solved</option></select> problem is the allocation of scarce resources. While some argue that immediate <select class="pte-select" data-answer="action"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="action">action</option><option value="acting">acting</option><option value="acted">acted</option><option value="actions">actions</option></select> is necessary, others believe that long-term strategies are more effective. Consequently, policymakers must <select class="pte-select" data-answer="strike"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="strike">strike</option><option value="striking">striking</option><option value="struck">struck</option><option value="strikes">strikes</option></select> a delicate balance between competing interests. Ultimately, the success of these initiatives will <select class="pte-select" data-answer="depend"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="depend">depend</option><option value="depending">depending</option><option value="depended">depended</option><option value="depends">depends</option></select> on international cooperation.',
