    'Phoenician traders <select class="pte-select" data-answer="developed"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="developing">developing</option><option value="develop">develop</option><option value="developed">developed</option><option value="develops">develops</option></select> a simplified writing system to record business transactions efficiently. Unlike earlier scripts, their alphabet used a small number of <select class="pte-select" data-answer="distinct"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="interchangeable">interchangeable</option><option value="matching">matching</option><option value="distinct">distinct</option><option value="identical">identical</option></select> symbols representing consonant sounds. Because Phoenician merchants traveled widely, their alphabet <select class="pte-select" data-answer="spread"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="vanished">vanished</option><option value="shrank">shrank</option><option value="spread">spread</option><option value="stagnated">stagnated</option></select> rapidly across the Mediterranean world. The Greeks later <select class="pte-select" data-answer="adapted"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="adapted">adapted</option><option value="adapting">adapting</option><option value="adapt">adapt</option><option value="adapts">adapts</option></select> this alphabet, adding vowels to create their own writing system.',
