    'The history of Ancient Egypt offers valuable insights into human resilience. Despite facing numerous <select class="pte-select" data-answer="hardships"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="hardships">hardships</option><option value="comforts">comforts</option><option value="luxuries">luxuries</option><option value="ease">ease</option></select>, these ancient people managed to thrive in harsh environments. Their agricultural practices were particularly <select class="pte-select" data-answer="ingenious"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="ingenious">ingenious</option><option value="foolish">foolish</option><option value="clumsy">clumsy</option><option value="destructive">destructive</option></select>, allowing them to sustain large populations. Additionally, their architectural <select class="pte-select" data-answer="marvels"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="marvels">marvels</option><option value="failures">failures</option><option value="disasters">disasters</option><option value="ruins">ruins</option></select> continue to astound modern engineers. Exploring their legacy helps us <select class="pte-select" data-answer="understand"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="understand">understand</option><option value="misunderstand">misunderstand</option><option value="ignore">ignore</option><option value="forget">forget</option></select> the complexities of early societies.',
    'Around 2500 BCE, the Indus Valley Civilization <select class="pte-select" data-answer="built"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="build">build</option><option value="building">building</option><option value="built">built</option><option value="builds">builds</option></select> large, carefully planned cities such as Harappa and Mohenjo-daro. These cities featured <select class="pte-select" data-answer="sophisticated"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="crude">crude</option><option value="sophisticated">sophisticated</option><option value="haphazard">haphazard</option><option value="primitive">primitive</option></select> drainage systems that were remarkably advanced for their time. Despite extensive archaeological study, the civilization\'s script remains <select class="pte-select" data-answer="undeciphered"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="clarified">clarified</option><option value="resolved">resolved</option><option value="undeciphered">undeciphered</option><option value="translated">translated</option></select>, leaving many questions unanswered. Around 1900 BCE, the civilization gradually <select class="pte-select" data-answer="declined"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="decline">decline</option><option value="declined">declined</option><option value="declines">declines</option><option value="declining">declining</option></select>, possibly due to climate change and shifting rivers.',
