    'Around 1000 CE, Norse explorer Leif Erikson <select class="pte-select" data-answer="undertook"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="undertake">undertake</option><option value="undertaking">undertaking</option><option value="undertook">undertook</option><option value="undertakes">undertakes</option></select> a voyage westward from Greenland. His expedition reached a forested land he named Vinland, praising its <select class="pte-select" data-answer="abundant"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="depleted">depleted</option><option value="scarce">scarce</option><option value="abundant">abundant</option><option value="meager">meager</option></select> resources such as timber and wild grapes. Archaeological evidence at L\'Anse aux Meadows in Newfoundland <select class="pte-select" data-answer="confirms"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="contradicts">contradicts</option><option value="confirms">confirms</option><option value="denies">denies</option><option value="disputes">disputes</option></select> that Norse settlers briefly lived in North America. The settlement was eventually <select class="pte-select" data-answer="abandoned"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="abandon">abandon</option><option value="abandoned">abandoned</option><option value="abandoning">abandoning</option><option value="abandons">abandons</option></select>, likely due to conflicts with indigenous peoples and long supply lines.',
