    'Cell biology has revealed that the plasma membrane <select class="pte-select" data-answer="forms"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="form">form</option><option value="forming">forming</option><option value="formed">formed</option><option value="forms">forms</option></select> a selectively permeable barrier around the cell. Water molecules move across this membrane through osmosis, always flowing toward the region of <select class="pte-select" data-answer="higher"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="equal">equal</option><option value="higher">higher</option><option value="minimal">minimal</option><option value="lower">lower</option></select> solute concentration. When a cell is placed in a hypertonic solution, it becomes <select class="pte-select" data-answer="shriveled"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="expanded">expanded</option><option value="swollen">swollen</option><option value="inflated">inflated</option><option value="shriveled">shriveled</option></select> as water exits and the cytoplasm shrinks. Maintaining proper osmotic balance is essential, or the cell will eventually <select class="pte-select" data-answer="rupture"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="rupturing">rupturing</option><option value="ruptured">ruptured</option><option value="ruptures">ruptures</option><option value="rupture">rupture</option></select> due to excessive water loss or gain.',
