    'Photosynthesis begins when chlorophyll molecules in the thylakoid membrane <select class="pte-select" data-answer="absorb"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="absorb">absorb</option><option value="absorbing">absorbing</option><option value="absorbs">absorbs</option><option value="absorbed">absorbed</option></select> light energy from the sun. This process also generates ATP and NADPH, which are <select class="pte-select" data-answer="essential"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="irrelevant">irrelevant</option><option value="harmful">harmful</option><option value="optional">optional</option><option value="essential">essential</option></select> for the subsequent synthesis of sugars. In the Calvin cycle, carbon dioxide is <select class="pte-select" data-answer="fixed"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="fixed">fixed</option><option value="fix">fix</option><option value="fixing">fixing</option><option value="fixes">fixes</option></select> into organic molecules using the energy stored in ATP. Without sufficient sunlight, this entire process becomes noticeably <select class="pte-select" data-answer="sluggish"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="swift">swift</option><option value="brisk">brisk</option><option value="sluggish">sluggish</option><option value="rapid">rapid</option></select>, resulting in reduced plant growth.',
