    'The human heart <select class="pte-select" data-answer="pumps"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="pumped">pumped</option><option value="pumps">pumps</option><option value="pump">pump</option><option value="pumping">pumping</option></select> blood throughout the body via a network of arteries, veins, and capillaries. Oxygen-rich blood leaves the left ventricle and travels through the aorta to reach <select class="pte-select" data-answer="peripheral"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="cardiac">cardiac</option><option value="central">central</option><option value="peripheral">peripheral</option><option value="internal">internal</option></select> tissues throughout the body. As blood passes through capillary beds, oxygen and nutrients move into surrounding cells through a process considered highly <select class="pte-select" data-answer="efficient"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="wasteful">wasteful</option><option value="efficient">efficient</option><option value="sluggish">sluggish</option><option value="negligible">negligible</option></select>, while waste products travel in the opposite direction. Deoxygenated blood then <select class="pte-select" data-answer="returns"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="returned">returned</option><option value="returns">returns</option><option value="return">return</option><option value="returning">returning</option></select> to the right atrium before being pumped to the lungs for reoxygenation.',
