    'When a pathogen invades the body, the immune system launches a highly <select class="pte-select" data-answer="targeted"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="random">random</option><option value="generic">generic</option><option value="broad">broad</option><option value="targeted">targeted</option></select> defensive response to eliminate it. B cells produce antibodies that <select class="pte-select" data-answer="bind"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="bound">bound</option><option value="binds">binds</option><option value="binding">binding</option><option value="bind">bind</option></select> specifically to antigens on the surface of the invading pathogen. This antigen recognition is remarkably <select class="pte-select" data-answer="precise"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="careless">careless</option><option value="precise">precise</option><option value="vague">vague</option><option value="random">random</option></select>, allowing the immune system to distinguish between millions of different pathogens. After the infection clears, memory cells <select class="pte-select" data-answer="persist"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="persists">persists</option><option value="persisted">persisted</option><option value="persisting">persisting</option><option value="persist">persist</option></select> in the body, providing faster protection against future exposure.',
    'Transpiration is the process by which water <select class="pte-select" data-answer="evaporates"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="evaporate">evaporate</option><option value="evaporated">evaporated</option><option value="evaporates">evaporates</option><option value="evaporating">evaporating</option></select> from the leaves of a plant into the atmosphere. This water loss creates a negative pressure that <select class="pte-select" data-answer="draws"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="draws">draws</option><option value="drawing">drawing</option><option value="drawn">drawn</option><option value="draw">draw</option></select> more water upward from the roots through the xylem. Stomata, tiny pores on the leaf surface, regulate this process and are <select class="pte-select" data-answer="sensitive"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="sensitive">sensitive</option><option value="indifferent">indifferent</option><option value="resistant">resistant</option><option value="immune">immune</option></select> to changes in humidity and light. During periods of drought, plants keep their stomata mostly <select class="pte-select" data-answer="shut"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="exposed">exposed</option><option value="open">open</option><option value="ajar">ajar</option><option value="shut">shut</option></select> to conserve water, slowing photosynthesis in the process.',
    'The evolution of marine biology has fundamentally changed how we understand the universe. Historically, researchers believed that consciousness <select class="pte-select" data-answer="played"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="plays">plays</option><option value="playing">playing</option><option value="played">played</option><option value="play">play</option></select> a minor role. However, recent studies have demonstrated that it is <select class="pte-select" data-answer="crucial"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="trivial">trivial</option><option value="crucial">crucial</option><option value="optional">optional</option><option value="irrelevant">irrelevant</option></select> to the overall system. As we continue to explore this fascinating field, it becomes <select class="pte-select" data-answer="evident"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="evident">evident</option><option value="obscure">obscure</option><option value="doubtful">doubtful</option><option value="hidden">hidden</option></select> that our previous models were incomplete. Future breakthroughs will likely <select class="pte-select" data-answer="rely"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="rely">rely</option><option value="relying">relying</option><option value="relied">relied</option><option value="relies">relies</option></select> heavily on interdisciplinary collaboration.',
    'At the height of summer the Patagonia, tourist ships move gently around the coast. Even 30 years ago such sights would have been unthinkable, but today people are willing to pay large sums of money to see the last real wilderness in the world. In the Southern Hemisphere, careless human exploitation <select class="pte-select" data-answer="has damaged"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="have damaged">have damaged</option><option value="has damaged">has damaged</option><option value="damaging">damaging</option><option value="damage">damage</option></select> the fragile ecosystem. Today concerned governments are trying to find ways to develop the region <select class="pte-select" data-answer="in the future"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="in the future">in the future</option><option value="ever before">ever before</option><option value="in the past">in the past</option><option value="on one side">on one side</option></select> special natural environment. <select class="pte-select" data-answer="Although"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="Because">Because</option><option value="Since">Since</option><option value="Although">Although</option><option value="When">When</option></select> the Patagonia is less accessible than the Southern Hemisphere, it is still largely undamaged by humans.',
    'At the height of summer the Galapagos, tourist ships move gently around the coast. Even 100 years ago such sights would have been unthinkable, but today people are willing to pay large sums of money to see the last real wilderness in the world. In the Ice Caps, careless human exploitation <select class="pte-select" data-answer="has damaged"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="have damaged">have damaged</option><option value="has damaged">has damaged</option><option value="damaging">damaging</option><option value="damage">damage</option></select> the fragile ecosystem. Today concerned governments are trying to find ways to develop the region <select class="pte-select" data-answer="in the future"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="in the future">in the future</option><option value="ever before">ever before</option><option value="in the past">in the past</option><option value="on one side">on one side</option></select> special natural environment. <select class="pte-select" data-answer="Although"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="Because">Because</option><option value="Since">Since</option><option value="Although">Although</option><option value="When">When</option></select> the Galapagos is less accessible than the Ice Caps, it is still largely undamaged by humans.',
    'Mitosis is the process by which a single cell <select class="pte-select" data-answer="divides"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="divide">divide</option><option value="divided">divided</option><option value="dividing">dividing</option><option value="divides">divides</option></select> into two genetically identical daughter cells. During prophase, chromosomes condense and become <select class="pte-select" data-answer="visible"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="transparent">transparent</option><option value="obscure">obscure</option><option value="visible">visible</option><option value="invisible">invisible</option></select> under a light microscope. In metaphase, spindle fibers <select class="pte-select" data-answer="align"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="aligned">aligned</option><option value="aligning">aligning</option><option value="align">align</option><option value="aligns">aligns</option></select> the chromosomes along the cell\'s equatorial plane. Finally, cytokinesis <select class="pte-select" data-answer="separates"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="separating">separating</option><option value="separates">separates</option><option value="separate">separate</option><option value="separated">separated</option></select> the cytoplasm, completing the formation of two separate cells.',
    'Predator and prey populations often follow <select class="pte-select" data-answer="cyclical"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="cyclical">cyclical</option><option value="random">random</option><option value="linear">linear</option><option value="static">static</option></select> patterns over time, as described by the Lotka-Volterra model. When prey become abundant, predator numbers <select class="pte-select" data-answer="rise"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="rise">rise</option><option value="rises">rises</option><option value="rising">rising</option><option value="risen">risen</option></select> as food becomes more readily available. However, this abundance of predators is only <select class="pte-select" data-answer="temporary"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="permanent">permanent</option><option value="stable">stable</option><option value="indefinite">indefinite</option><option value="temporary">temporary</option></select>, since prey populations eventually decline under increased predation pressure. Once prey numbers fall too low, predator populations <select class="pte-select" data-answer="decline"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="declined">declined</option><option value="decline">decline</option><option value="declining">declining</option><option value="declines">declines</option></select> as well, restarting the cycle.',
    'During translation, ribosomes interpret messenger RNA through a process that is remarkably <select class="pte-select" data-answer="universal"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="inconsistent">inconsistent</option><option value="rare">rare</option><option value="universal">universal</option><option value="limited">limited</option></select> across nearly all living organisms. Each three-nucleotide codon <select class="pte-select" data-answer="codes"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="coding">coding</option><option value="codes">codes</option><option value="code">code</option><option value="coded">coded</option></select> for a specific amino acid or a stop signal. Transfer RNA molecules bring amino acids to the ribosome in an order that is <select class="pte-select" data-answer="determined"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="determining">determining</option><option value="determined">determined</option><option value="determine">determine</option><option value="determines">determines</option></select> by the sequence of codons. Once the ribosome reaches a stop codon, translation is considered <select class="pte-select" data-answer="complete"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="ongoing">ongoing</option><option value="complete">complete</option><option value="incomplete">incomplete</option><option value="partial">partial</option></select>, and the completed protein chain is released.',
    'Digestion begins in the mouth, where the enzyme amylase <select class="pte-select" data-answer="initiates"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="initiated">initiated</option><option value="initiating">initiating</option><option value="initiate">initiate</option><option value="initiates">initiates</option></select> the breakdown of starch into simpler sugars. In the stomach, pepsin works best in a highly <select class="pte-select" data-answer="acidic"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="basic">basic</option><option value="alkaline">alkaline</option><option value="neutral">neutral</option><option value="acidic">acidic</option></select> environment created by hydrochloric acid. The small intestine then plays the most <select class="pte-select" data-answer="significant"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="negligible">negligible</option><option value="significant">significant</option><option value="trivial">trivial</option><option value="minor">minor</option></select> role in absorbing nutrients into the bloodstream. Without these digestive enzymes, the body would be unable to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="extract"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="extracted">extracted</option><option value="extracting">extracting</option><option value="extract">extract</option><option value="extracts">extracts</option></select> the nutrients it needs to survive.',
    'Charles Darwin\'s observations of finches on the Galápagos Islands proved <select class="pte-select" data-answer="instrumental"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="harmful">harmful</option><option value="negligible">negligible</option><option value="instrumental">instrumental</option><option value="irrelevant">irrelevant</option></select> to the development of his theory of evolution by natural selection. He noticed that finches on different islands had beak shapes <select class="pte-select" data-answer="precisely"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="poorly">poorly</option><option value="randomly">randomly</option><option value="precisely">precisely</option><option value="vaguely">vaguely</option></select> suited to their specific food sources. Individuals with beak shapes better adapted to their environment were more likely to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="survive"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="survives">survives</option><option value="survived">survived</option><option value="survive">survive</option><option value="surviving">surviving</option></select> and reproduce. Over many generations, these advantageous traits <select class="pte-select" data-answer="accumulated"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="accumulate">accumulate</option><option value="accumulates">accumulates</option><option value="accumulated">accumulated</option><option value="accumulating">accumulating</option></select> within the population, leading to the formation of new species.',
    'Mitochondria are often described as the powerhouse of the cell because they <select class="pte-select" data-answer="generate"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="generated">generated</option><option value="generate">generate</option><option value="generating">generating</option><option value="generates">generates</option></select> most of the ATP a cell needs. During cellular respiration, glucose is broken down in a series of reactions that release <select class="pte-select" data-answer="substantial"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="minimal">minimal</option><option value="trivial">trivial</option><option value="substantial">substantial</option><option value="negligible">negligible</option></select> amounts of usable energy. The final stage, oxidative phosphorylation, is highly <select class="pte-select" data-answer="dependent"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="immune">immune</option><option value="indifferent">indifferent</option><option value="independent">independent</option><option value="dependent">dependent</option></select> on oxygen as the final electron acceptor in the electron transport chain. Without sufficient oxygen, cells must instead <select class="pte-select" data-answer="switch"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="switch">switch</option><option value="switching">switching</option><option value="switched">switched</option><option value="switches">switches</option></select> to anaerobic respiration, producing far less energy.',
    'In the lungs, tiny air sacs called alveoli <select class="pte-select" data-answer="maximize"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="maximizes">maximizes</option><option value="maximizing">maximizing</option><option value="maximized">maximized</option><option value="maximize">maximize</option></select> the surface area available for gas exchange. Oxygen diffuses across the thin alveolar walls into the blood, while carbon dioxide moves in the <select class="pte-select" data-answer="opposite"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="identical">identical</option><option value="same">same</option><option value="opposite">opposite</option><option value="forward">forward</option></select> direction. This exchange is <select class="pte-select" data-answer="highly"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="poorly">poorly</option><option value="barely">barely</option><option value="highly">highly</option><option value="rarely">rarely</option></select> efficient because alveoli are surrounded by dense networks of capillaries. During exercise, the breathing rate <select class="pte-select" data-answer="increases"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="increase">increase</option><option value="increased">increased</option><option value="increases">increases</option><option value="increasing">increasing</option></select> to supply working muscles with additional oxygen.',
    'Symbiotic relationships in nature <select class="pte-select" data-answer="take"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="taking">taking</option><option value="took">took</option><option value="take">take</option><option value="takes">takes</option></select> several distinct forms, including mutualism, commensalism, and parasitism. In a mutualistic relationship, both species gain a <select class="pte-select" data-answer="mutual"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="mutual">mutual</option><option value="doubtful">doubtful</option><option value="minimal">minimal</option><option value="one-sided">one-sided</option></select> advantage from their interaction. The relationship between bees and flowering plants is a <select class="pte-select" data-answer="classic"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="dubious">dubious</option><option value="questionable">questionable</option><option value="rare">rare</option><option value="classic">classic</option></select> example of mutualism, as bees gain nectar while plants achieve pollination. In contrast, parasitic relationships <select class="pte-select" data-answer="benefit"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="benefit">benefit</option><option value="benefiting">benefiting</option><option value="benefited">benefited</option><option value="benefits">benefits</option></select> one organism at the expense of another.',
    'Stem cells are unique in that they retain a remarkably <select class="pte-select" data-answer="flexible"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="fixed">fixed</option><option value="rigid">rigid</option><option value="flexible">flexible</option><option value="limited">limited</option></select> capacity to develop into many different cell types. Embryonic stem cells are considered <select class="pte-select" data-answer="pluripotent"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="pluripotent">pluripotent</option><option value="unipotent">unipotent</option><option value="totipotent">totipotent</option><option value="multipotent">multipotent</option></select>, meaning they can differentiate into almost any cell type in the body. As development proceeds, these cells gradually <select class="pte-select" data-answer="differentiate"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="differentiated">differentiated</option><option value="differentiate">differentiate</option><option value="differentiates">differentiates</option><option value="differentiating">differentiating</option></select> into specialized types such as neurons or muscle cells. Scientists hope that stem cell therapies will eventually <select class="pte-select" data-answer="repair"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="repaired">repaired</option><option value="repairing">repairing</option><option value="repairs">repairs</option><option value="repair">repair</option></select> damaged tissues in patients with degenerative diseases.',
    'The introduction of the Intel 4004 in 1971 <select class="pte-select" data-answer="marked"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="marks">marks</option><option value="marked">marked</option><option value="marking">marking</option><option value="mark">mark</option></select> a turning point in the history of computing. This single chip <select class="pte-select" data-answer="consolidated"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="isolated">isolated</option><option value="consolidated">consolidated</option><option value="fragmented">fragmented</option><option value="duplicated">duplicated</option></select> the functions of an entire central processing unit onto one piece of silicon. Engineers had long <select class="pte-select" data-answer="predicted"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="predict">predict</option><option value="predicting">predicting</option><option value="predicted">predicted</option><option value="predicts">predicts</option></select> that shrinking transistors would eventually allow such integration. Today, microprocessors remain <select class="pte-select" data-answer="essential"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="essential">essential</option><option value="incidental">incidental</option><option value="optional">optional</option><option value="peripheral">peripheral</option></select> to virtually every electronic device we use.',
    'Recent advancements in nanotechnology have paved the way for innovative applications. For instance, the use of <select class="pte-select" data-answer="sophisticated"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="sophisticated">sophisticated</option><option value="simple">simple</option><option value="outdated">outdated</option><option value="basic">basic</option></select> algorithms has optimized data processing speeds. Furthermore, these systems are <select class="pte-select" data-answer="increasingly"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="increasingly">increasingly</option><option value="decreasingly">decreasingly</option><option value="rarely">rarely</option><option value="never">never</option></select> capable of learning from vast amounts of information without explicit programming. Critics, however, <select class="pte-select" data-answer="raise"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="raise">raise</option><option value="raises">raises</option><option value="raising">raising</option><option value="raised">raised</option></select> concerns regarding data privacy and security. Addressing these ethical issues remains a <select class="pte-select" data-answer="top"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="top">top</option><option value="bottom">bottom</option><option value="low">low</option><option value="zero">zero</option></select> priority for developers.',
    'At the height of summer the Patagonia, tourist ships move gently around the coast. Even 10 years ago such sights would have been unthinkable, but today people are willing to pay large sums of money to see the last real wilderness in the world. In the Northern Hemisphere, careless human exploitation <select class="pte-select" data-answer="has damaged"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="have damaged">have damaged</option><option value="has damaged">has damaged</option><option value="damaging">damaging</option><option value="damage">damage</option></select> the fragile ecosystem. Today concerned governments are trying to find ways to develop the region <select class="pte-select" data-answer="in the future"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="in the future">in the future</option><option value="ever before">ever before</option><option value="in the past">in the past</option><option value="on one side">on one side</option></select> special natural environment. <select class="pte-select" data-answer="Although"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="Because">Because</option><option value="Since">Since</option><option value="Although">Although</option><option value="When">When</option></select> the Patagonia is less accessible than the Northern Hemisphere, it is still largely undamaged by humans.',
    'In 1989, Tim Berners-Lee <select class="pte-select" data-answer="submitted"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="submitted">submitted</option><option value="submit">submit</option><option value="submitting">submitting</option><option value="submits">submits</option></select> a proposal for a global hypertext system while working at CERN. His invention, the World Wide Web, made it possible to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="link"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="link">link</option><option value="fragment">fragment</option><option value="isolate">isolate</option><option value="conceal">conceal</option></select> documents across different computers via hyperlinks. By the mid-1990s, graphical browsers such as Mosaic had <select class="pte-select" data-answer="made"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="makes">makes</option><option value="made">made</option><option value="making">making</option><option value="make">make</option></select> the web accessible to ordinary users. The web\'s rapid growth soon proved <select class="pte-select" data-answer="transformative"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="superficial">superficial</option><option value="detrimental">detrimental</option><option value="transformative">transformative</option><option value="negligible">negligible</option></select> to commerce, education, and communication worldwide.',
    'In supervised learning, an algorithm <select class="pte-select" data-answer="learns"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="learned">learned</option><option value="learn">learn</option><option value="learning">learning</option><option value="learns">learns</option></select> patterns from a dataset containing labeled examples. Each labeled example <select class="pte-select" data-answer="guides"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="obstructs">obstructs</option><option value="confuses">confuses</option><option value="guides">guides</option><option value="misleads">misleads</option></select> the algorithm toward the correct output during training. Once training is complete, the model <select class="pte-select" data-answer="is"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="be">be</option><option value="is">is</option><option value="been">been</option><option value="are">are</option></select> evaluated on a separate test set to measure accuracy. This process helps determine whether the algorithm can <select class="pte-select" data-answer="generalize"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="restrict">restrict</option><option value="memorize">memorize</option><option value="duplicate">duplicate</option><option value="generalize">generalize</option></select> well to new, unseen data.',
    'Industrial robots first <select class="pte-select" data-answer="found"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="find">find</option><option value="finding">finding</option><option value="found">found</option><option value="finds">finds</option></select> widespread use on automobile assembly lines during the 1960s. These early machines could <select class="pte-select" data-answer="perform"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="perform">perform</option><option value="postpone">postpone</option><option value="evaluate">evaluate</option><option value="abandon">abandon</option></select> repetitive tasks such as welding and painting with great precision. Modern robots are typically <select class="pte-select" data-answer="equipped"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="equipped">equipped</option><option value="equipping">equipping</option><option value="equips">equips</option><option value="equip">equip</option></select> with sensors that allow them to react to their environment. As robotics technology advances, collaborative robots increasingly <select class="pte-select" data-answer="operate"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="operate">operate</option><option value="compete">compete</option><option value="collide">collide</option><option value="interfere">interfere</option></select> alongside human workers rather than replacing them.',
    'Fiber optic cables <select class="pte-select" data-answer="were"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="are">are</option><option value="being">being</option><option value="were">were</option><option value="was">was</option></select> developed in the 1970s as a faster alternative to copper wiring. These cables <select class="pte-select" data-answer="transmit"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="transmit">transmit</option><option value="distort">distort</option><option value="absorb">absorb</option><option value="obstruct">obstruct</option></select> data as pulses of light rather than electrical signals. Because light travels with minimal signal loss, fiber optics can <select class="pte-select" data-answer="carry"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="carries">carries</option><option value="carry">carry</option><option value="carried">carried</option><option value="carrying">carrying</option></select> information over much longer distances than copper. Today, fiber optic networks form the <select class="pte-select" data-answer="backbone"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="surface">surface</option><option value="boundary">boundary</option><option value="backbone">backbone</option><option value="perimeter">perimeter</option></select> of global internet infrastructure.',
