    'Linus Torvalds <select class="pte-select" data-answer="created"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="create">create</option><option value="created">created</option><option value="creates">creates</option><option value="creating">creating</option></select> Git in 2005 to manage the development of the Linux kernel. Unlike earlier centralized systems, Git allows each developer to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="maintain"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="conceal">conceal</option><option value="maintain">maintain</option><option value="abandon">abandon</option><option value="erase">erase</option></select> a complete copy of the project\'s history. Developers <select class="pte-select" data-answer="record"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="records">records</option><option value="recording">recording</option><option value="recorded">recorded</option><option value="record">record</option></select> changes to a repository through a process called committing. This distributed approach makes it easier to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="reconcile"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="ignore">ignore</option><option value="reconcile">reconcile</option><option value="amplify">amplify</option><option value="duplicate">duplicate</option></select> conflicting changes made by multiple contributors.',
