    'The term El Niño <select class="pte-select" data-answer="describes"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="describes">describes</option><option value="describe">describe</option><option value="described">described</option><option value="describing">describing</option></select> a periodic warming of surface waters in the central and eastern Pacific Ocean. During an El Niño event, trade winds <select class="pte-select" data-answer="weaken"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="weakened">weakened</option><option value="weakening">weakening</option><option value="weaken">weaken</option><option value="weakens">weakens</option></select> and sometimes reverse direction. This shift can <select class="pte-select" data-answer="cause"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="cause">cause</option><option value="delay">delay</option><option value="resolve">resolve</option><option value="prevent">prevent</option></select> severe droughts in some regions and flooding in others. Meteorologists closely <select class="pte-select" data-answer="monitor"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="disregard">disregard</option><option value="abandon">abandon</option><option value="conceal">conceal</option><option value="monitor">monitor</option></select> ocean temperatures to predict the onset of these events.',
