    'Aquifers <select class="pte-select" data-answer="store"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="stored">stored</option><option value="stores">stores</option><option value="store">store</option><option value="storing">storing</option></select> significant quantities of water within underground layers of rock or sediment. Farmers and cities increasingly <select class="pte-select" data-answer="rely"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="relied">relied</option><option value="relies">relies</option><option value="rely">rely</option><option value="relying">relying</option></select> on these reserves for irrigation and drinking water. In many regions, water is being withdrawn far faster than it can naturally <select class="pte-select" data-answer="replenish"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="disperse">disperse</option><option value="evaporate">evaporate</option><option value="replenish">replenish</option><option value="contaminate">contaminate</option></select>. This overuse can lead to land subsidence and permanently <select class="pte-select" data-answer="lowered"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="lowered">lowered</option><option value="purified">purified</option><option value="elevated">elevated</option><option value="raised">raised</option></select> water tables.',
    'Cities frequently <select class="pte-select" data-answer="experience"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="experiencing">experiencing</option><option value="experiences">experiences</option><option value="experience">experience</option><option value="experienced">experienced</option></select> significantly higher temperatures than surrounding rural areas. This phenomenon, known as the urban heat island effect, <select class="pte-select" data-answer="results"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="resulting">resulting</option><option value="results">results</option><option value="resulted">resulted</option><option value="result">result</option></select> largely from concrete and asphalt absorbing heat. Dark, impervious surfaces <select class="pte-select" data-answer="absorb"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="repel">repel</option><option value="absorb">absorb</option><option value="deflect">deflect</option><option value="scatter">scatter</option></select> solar radiation instead of reflecting it away. Planting trees and adding green roofs can help <select class="pte-select" data-answer="lower"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="elevate">elevate</option><option value="stabilize">stabilize</option><option value="ignore">ignore</option><option value="lower">lower</option></select> urban temperatures.',
    'Johannes Kepler <select class="pte-select" data-answer="formulated"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="formulated">formulated</option><option value="formulates">formulates</option><option value="formulate">formulate</option><option value="formulating">formulating</option></select> three laws that describe the motion of planets around the sun. His first law states that planets move in elliptical, not circular, <select class="pte-select" data-answer="orbits"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="loops">loops</option><option value="spirals">spirals</option><option value="circuits">circuits</option><option value="orbits">orbits</option></select>. According to his second law, a planet sweeps out equal areas in equal <select class="pte-select" data-answer="intervals"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="intervals">intervals</option><option value="periods">periods</option><option value="spans">spans</option><option value="durations">durations</option></select> of time. The third law <select class="pte-select" data-answer="relates"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="relating">relating</option><option value="related">related</option><option value="relates">relates</option><option value="relate">relate</option></select> the orbital period of a planet to its distance from the sun.',
    'The solar system <select class="pte-select" data-answer="formed"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="forming">forming</option><option value="formed">formed</option><option value="form">form</option><option value="forms">forms</option></select> approximately 4.6 billion years ago from a rotating cloud of gas and dust. Gravity caused the material in this solar nebula to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="collapse"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="disperse">disperse</option><option value="expand">expand</option><option value="collapse">collapse</option><option value="scatter">scatter</option></select> toward its center. Most of the mass concentrated at the center, eventually forming the <select class="pte-select" data-answer="protostar"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="protostar">protostar</option><option value="comet">comet</option><option value="nebula">nebula</option><option value="asteroid">asteroid</option></select>. The remaining debris gradually <select class="pte-select" data-answer="coalesced"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="coalesces">coalesces</option><option value="coalescing">coalescing</option><option value="coalesced">coalesced</option><option value="coalesce">coalesce</option></select> into planets, moons, and asteroids.',
    'A black hole <select class="pte-select" data-answer="forms"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="forms">forms</option><option value="forming">forming</option><option value="form">form</option><option value="formed">formed</option></select> when a massive star collapses under its own gravity at the end of its life. Its gravitational pull is so <select class="pte-select" data-answer="intense"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="mild">mild</option><option value="intense">intense</option><option value="moderate">moderate</option><option value="faint">faint</option></select> that not even light can escape beyond a certain boundary. This boundary, known as the event horizon, marks the point of no <select class="pte-select" data-answer="return"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="return">return</option><option value="contact">contact</option><option value="motion">motion</option><option value="entry">entry</option></select>. Scientists <select class="pte-select" data-answer="detect"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="detecting">detecting</option><option value="detected">detected</option><option value="detect">detect</option><option value="detects">detects</option></select> black holes indirectly by observing their effects on nearby stars and gas.',
