    'Most scientists believe the Moon <select class="pte-select" data-answer="formed"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="forms">forms</option><option value="form">form</option><option value="forming">forming</option><option value="formed">formed</option></select> from debris left after a massive collision. According to this hypothesis, a Mars-sized body <select class="pte-select" data-answer="crashed"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="crashing">crashing</option><option value="crashed">crashed</option><option value="crash">crash</option><option value="crashes">crashes</option></select> into the early Earth billions of years ago. The resulting debris eventually gathered together and <select class="pte-select" data-answer="coalesced"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="vanished">vanished</option><option value="coalesced">coalesced</option><option value="scattered">scattered</option><option value="dissolved">dissolved</option></select> into the Moon we see today. This impact theory helps explain why the Moon\'s composition is remarkably <select class="pte-select" data-answer="similar"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="opposite">opposite</option><option value="unrelated">unrelated</option><option value="foreign">foreign</option><option value="similar">similar</option></select> to Earth\'s outer layers.',
    'Saturn\'s rings are <select class="pte-select" data-answer="composed"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="composes">composes</option><option value="composed">composed</option><option value="composing">composing</option><option value="compose">compose</option></select> almost entirely of ice particles mixed with smaller amounts of rock. The rings are extremely wide but remarkably <select class="pte-select" data-answer="thin"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="solid">solid</option><option value="thick">thick</option><option value="thin">thin</option><option value="dense">dense</option></select>, often only about ten meters deep. Scientists believe the rings may have <select class="pte-select" data-answer="originated"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="originated">originated</option><option value="originate">originate</option><option value="originates">originates</option><option value="originating">originating</option></select> from a shattered moon or comet. The Cassini spacecraft <select class="pte-select" data-answer="gathered"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="gather">gather</option><option value="gathers">gathers</option><option value="gathering">gathering</option><option value="gathered">gathered</option></select> valuable data about the rings\' structure before its mission ended.',
    'The cosmic microwave background is the faint radiation that <select class="pte-select" data-answer="remains"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="remained">remained</option><option value="remain">remain</option><option value="remains">remains</option><option value="remaining">remaining</option></select> from the early universe. It was first <select class="pte-select" data-answer="accidentally"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="purposefully">purposefully</option><option value="deliberately">deliberately</option><option value="intentionally">intentionally</option><option value="accidentally">accidentally</option></select> discovered in 1965 by two radio astronomers. This radiation provides strong <select class="pte-select" data-answer="evidence"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="resistance">resistance</option><option value="evidence">evidence</option><option value="doubt">doubt</option><option value="confusion">confusion</option></select> supporting the Big Bang theory. Its nearly uniform temperature across the sky <select class="pte-select" data-answer="reveals"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="reveal">reveal</option><option value="reveals">reveals</option><option value="revealing">revealing</option><option value="revealed">revealed</option></select> how evenly matter was distributed long ago.',
    'A supernova <select class="pte-select" data-answer="occurs"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="occurred">occurred</option><option value="occurring">occurring</option><option value="occurs">occurs</option><option value="occur">occur</option></select> when a star reaches the explosive end of its life cycle. Type Ia supernovae happen when a white dwarf <select class="pte-select" data-answer="accretes"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="accreted">accreted</option><option value="accrete">accrete</option><option value="accreting">accreting</option><option value="accretes">accretes</option></select> material from a companion star until it becomes unstable. Type II supernovae result from the <select class="pte-select" data-answer="sudden"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="slow">slow</option><option value="sudden">sudden</option><option value="gradual">gradual</option><option value="steady">steady</option></select> collapse of a massive star\'s core. These explosions <select class="pte-select" data-answer="scatter"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="scatter">scatter</option><option value="scattering">scattering</option><option value="scatters">scatters</option><option value="scattered">scattered</option></select> heavy elements throughout the galaxy, enriching future generations of stars.',
