    'NASA\'s rovers have <select class="pte-select" data-answer="explored"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="explore">explore</option><option value="explored">explored</option><option value="explores">explores</option><option value="exploring">exploring</option></select> the Martian surface for signs of past water activity. The Perseverance rover <select class="pte-select" data-answer="collects"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="collecting">collecting</option><option value="collects">collects</option><option value="collected">collected</option><option value="collect">collect</option></select> rock samples that may later be returned to Earth. Images from the rovers reveal a landscape that was once <select class="pte-select" data-answer="significantly"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="negligibly">negligibly</option><option value="barely">barely</option><option value="significantly">significantly</option><option value="marginally">marginally</option></select> wetter than it is today. Data gathered by these missions continues to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="reshape"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="reshaped">reshaped</option><option value="reshape">reshape</option><option value="reshaping">reshaping</option><option value="reshapes">reshapes</option></select> our understanding of Mars\'s ancient climate.',
    'Dark matter is a mysterious substance that does not <select class="pte-select" data-answer="emit"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="emits">emits</option><option value="emitted">emitted</option><option value="emit">emit</option><option value="emitting">emitting</option></select> light or other electromagnetic radiation. Its presence is inferred from its gravitational <select class="pte-select" data-answer="effect"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="distortion">distortion</option><option value="delay">delay</option><option value="effect">effect</option><option value="absence">absence</option></select> on visible galaxies and stars. Without dark matter, galaxies would not have enough mass to remain <select class="pte-select" data-answer="bound"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="bound">bound</option><option value="isolated">isolated</option><option value="separated">separated</option><option value="scattered">scattered</option></select> together. Researchers continue to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="seek"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="seek">seek</option><option value="ignore">ignore</option><option value="avoid">avoid</option><option value="abandon">abandon</option></select> dark matter using highly sensitive underground detectors.',
