    'Galileo Galilei <select class="pte-select" data-answer="used"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="using">using</option><option value="use">use</option><option value="uses">uses</option><option value="used">used</option></select> a telescope to study the night sky in the early seventeenth century. Through his observations, he <select class="pte-select" data-answer="discovered"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="discovers">discovers</option><option value="discovering">discovering</option><option value="discover">discover</option><option value="discovered">discovered</option></select> four large moons orbiting Jupiter. This finding was <select class="pte-select" data-answer="significant"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="trivial">trivial</option><option value="ordinary">ordinary</option><option value="irrelevant">irrelevant</option><option value="significant">significant</option></select> because it showed that not everything orbits the Earth. Galileo\'s evidence eventually helped <select class="pte-select" data-answer="overturn"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="overturning">overturning</option><option value="overturns">overturns</option><option value="overturned">overturned</option><option value="overturn">overturn</option></select> the Earth-centered model of the universe.',
