    'As a star like the sun ages, it eventually <select class="pte-select" data-answer="exhausts"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="exhaust">exhaust</option><option value="exhausted">exhausted</option><option value="exhausts">exhausts</option><option value="exhausting">exhausting</option></select> its supply of hydrogen fuel. The star\'s core then contracts while its outer layers <select class="pte-select" data-answer="expand"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="shrink">shrink</option><option value="expand">expand</option><option value="cool">cool</option><option value="vanish">vanish</option></select> dramatically. This process transforms the star into a <select class="pte-select" data-answer="red"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="yellow">yellow</option><option value="red">red</option><option value="white">white</option><option value="blue">blue</option></select> giant, many times larger than before. Eventually, the outer layers will drift away, leaving behind a small, dense <select class="pte-select" data-answer="white"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="black">black</option><option value="white">white</option><option value="blue">blue</option><option value="red">red</option></select> dwarf.',
