    'The evolution of cognitive psychology has fundamentally changed how we understand the cellular structure. Historically, researchers believed that DNA <select class="pte-select" data-answer="played"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="plays">plays</option><option value="playing">playing</option><option value="played">played</option><option value="play">play</option></select> a minor role. However, recent studies have demonstrated that it is <select class="pte-select" data-answer="crucial"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="trivial">trivial</option><option value="crucial">crucial</option><option value="optional">optional</option><option value="irrelevant">irrelevant</option></select> to the overall system. As we continue to explore this fascinating field, it becomes <select class="pte-select" data-answer="evident"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="evident">evident</option><option value="obscure">obscure</option><option value="doubtful">doubtful</option><option value="hidden">hidden</option></select> that our previous models were incomplete. Future breakthroughs will likely <select class="pte-select" data-answer="rely"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="rely">rely</option><option value="relying">relying</option><option value="relied">relied</option><option value="relies">relies</option></select> heavily on interdisciplinary collaboration.',
