    'Mirror neurons are a class of brain cells that <select class="pte-select" data-answer="show"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="showing">showing</option><option value="showed">showed</option><option value="show">show</option><option value="shows">shows</option></select> activity both when an individual performs an action and when they observe another performing it. These neurons were first <select class="pte-select" data-answer="identified"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="identified">identified</option><option value="forbidden">forbidden</option><option value="eliminated">eliminated</option><option value="disputed">disputed</option></select> in the premotor cortex of macaque monkeys during the 1990s. Some researchers believe mirror neurons may <select class="pte-select" data-answer="play"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="plays">plays</option><option value="played">played</option><option value="playing">playing</option><option value="play">play</option></select> a role in empathy and social understanding. However, the precise function of these neurons in humans <select class="pte-select" data-answer="remains"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="remains">remains</option><option value="concludes">concludes</option><option value="vanishes">vanishes</option><option value="resolves">resolves</option></select> a topic of ongoing scientific debate.',
    'Stanley Milgram designed a controversial experiment to examine how far ordinary people would <select class="pte-select" data-answer="obey"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="obeying">obeying</option><option value="obey">obey</option><option value="obeyed">obeyed</option><option value="obeys">obeys</option></select> instructions from an authority figure. Participants were told to administer what they believed were increasingly <select class="pte-select" data-answer="painful"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="pleasant">pleasant</option><option value="harmless">harmless</option><option value="negligible">negligible</option><option value="painful">painful</option></select> electric shocks to another person. Surprisingly, a majority of participants <select class="pte-select" data-answer="continued"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="continued">continued</option><option value="continue">continue</option><option value="continuing">continuing</option><option value="continues">continues</option></select> to the highest voltage level despite audible distress from the supposed victim. The study has since <select class="pte-select" data-answer="sparked"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="prevented">prevented</option><option value="concluded">concluded</option><option value="resolved">resolved</option><option value="sparked">sparked</option></select> significant ethical debate about the treatment of research participants.',
    'Psychologist Carol Dweck proposed that individuals generally <select class="pte-select" data-answer="hold"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="hold">hold</option><option value="held">held</option><option value="holding">holding</option><option value="holds">holds</option></select> one of two core beliefs about their own abilities. Those with a fixed mindset believe intelligence is largely <select class="pte-select" data-answer="innate"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="temporary">temporary</option><option value="innate">innate</option><option value="negotiable">negotiable</option><option value="flexible">flexible</option></select> and cannot be significantly changed. In contrast, people with a growth mindset believe abilities can <select class="pte-select" data-answer="improve"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="improves">improves</option><option value="improve">improve</option><option value="improving">improving</option><option value="improved">improved</option></select> through effort and practice. Research suggests that fostering a growth mindset can <select class="pte-select" data-answer="enhance"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="ignore">ignore</option><option value="diminish">diminish</option><option value="enhance">enhance</option><option value="delay">delay</option></select> students\' motivation and resilience in the face of setbacks.',
    'Anchoring bias occurs when people rely too heavily on the first piece of information they <select class="pte-select" data-answer="encounter"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="encounter">encounter</option><option value="encounters">encounters</option><option value="encountering">encountering</option><option value="encountered">encountered</option></select> when making a decision. This initial value, or anchor, can <select class="pte-select" data-answer="distort"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="confirm">confirm</option><option value="clarify">clarify</option><option value="verify">verify</option><option value="distort">distort</option></select> subsequent judgments even when it is arbitrary or irrelevant. In one experiment, participants who saw a higher number <select class="pte-select" data-answer="gave"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="gives">gives</option><option value="giving">giving</option><option value="gave">gave</option><option value="give">give</option></select> estimates that were consistently higher than those who saw a lower number. Negotiators often exploit this bias by <select class="pte-select" data-answer="proposing"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="proposing">proposing</option><option value="propose">propose</option><option value="proposes">proposes</option><option value="proposed">proposed</option></select> an initial offer that is deliberately extreme.',
    'Adam Smith\'s 1776 treatise argued that individuals pursuing their own self-interest <select class="pte-select" data-answer="enrich"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="enriching">enriching</option><option value="enriches">enriches</option><option value="enrich">enrich</option><option value="enriched">enriched</option></select> the wealth of nations as a whole. He famously described an "invisible hand" that guides markets toward outcomes that are <select class="pte-select" data-answer="beneficial"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="beneficial">beneficial</option><option value="arbitrary">arbitrary</option><option value="negligible">negligible</option><option value="detrimental">detrimental</option></select> to society, even without central planning. Critics have long argued that unregulated markets can also produce outcomes that are deeply <select class="pte-select" data-answer="harmful"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="harmless">harmless</option><option value="harmful">harmful</option><option value="admirable">admirable</option><option value="flawless">flawless</option></select>, such as monopolies and pollution. Nevertheless, Smith\'s ideas continue to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="shape"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="shape">shape</option><option value="shapes">shapes</option><option value="shaped">shaped</option><option value="shaping">shaping</option></select> modern debates about the proper role of government in the economy.',
    'Economists use the model of supply and demand to explain how prices <select class="pte-select" data-answer="adjust"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="adjusts">adjusts</option><option value="adjusted">adjusted</option><option value="adjust">adjust</option><option value="adjusting">adjusting</option></select> in competitive markets. When demand for a product rises while supply stays constant, the price typically becomes more <select class="pte-select" data-answer="expensive"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="expensive">expensive</option><option value="uniform">uniform</option><option value="negligible">negligible</option><option value="affordable">affordable</option></select>. Conversely, a surplus of goods often forces sellers to lower prices until the market reaches <select class="pte-select" data-answer="equilibrium"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="turbulence">turbulence</option><option value="equilibrium">equilibrium</option><option value="isolation">isolation</option><option value="stagnation">stagnation</option></select>. Governments sometimes <select class="pte-select" data-answer="impose"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="imposed">imposed</option><option value="imposes">imposes</option><option value="impose">impose</option><option value="imposing">imposing</option></select> price controls, such as rent caps, to protect consumers from sudden spikes.',
    'Central banks such as the Federal Reserve are tasked with keeping inflation <select class="pte-select" data-answer="stable"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="arbitrary">arbitrary</option><option value="negligible">negligible</option><option value="stable">stable</option><option value="volatile">volatile</option></select> over the long term. To slow rising prices, a central bank typically <select class="pte-select" data-answer="raises"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="raise">raise</option><option value="raising">raising</option><option value="raised">raised</option><option value="raises">raises</option></select> interest rates, making borrowing more expensive. Higher interest rates tend to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="reduce"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="reduces">reduces</option><option value="reduce">reduce</option><option value="reducing">reducing</option><option value="reduced">reduced</option></select> consumer spending and business investment. If rates rise too quickly, however, the economy may face a serious <select class="pte-select" data-answer="recession"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="expansion">expansion</option><option value="boom">boom</option><option value="surplus">surplus</option><option value="recession">recession</option></select>.',
    'At the height of summer the Patagonia, tourist ships move gently around the coast. Even 30 years ago such sights would have been unthinkable, but today people are willing to pay large sums of money to see the last real wilderness in the world. In the Tundra, careless human exploitation <select class="pte-select" data-answer="has damaged"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="have damaged">have damaged</option><option value="has damaged">has damaged</option><option value="damaging">damaging</option><option value="damage">damage</option></select> the fragile ecosystem. Today concerned governments are trying to find ways to develop the region <select class="pte-select" data-answer="in the future"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="in the future">in the future</option><option value="ever before">ever before</option><option value="in the past">in the past</option><option value="on one side">on one side</option></select> special natural environment. <select class="pte-select" data-answer="Although"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="Because">Because</option><option value="Since">Since</option><option value="Although">Although</option><option value="When">When</option></select> the Patagonia is less accessible than the Tundra, it is still largely undamaged by humans.',
    'In 1944, representatives from 44 nations met at Bretton Woods to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="establish"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="establishes">establishes</option><option value="establish">establish</option><option value="establishing">establishing</option><option value="established">established</option></select> a new international monetary system. The agreement pegged currencies to the US dollar, which was in turn <select class="pte-select" data-answer="convertible"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="adjustable">adjustable</option><option value="transferable">transferable</option><option value="convertible">convertible</option><option value="negotiable">negotiable</option></select> to gold at a fixed rate. This system provided <select class="pte-select" data-answer="fixed"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="fixed">fixed</option><option value="arbitrary">arbitrary</option><option value="floating">floating</option><option value="erratic">erratic</option></select> exchange rates that helped stabilize international trade for nearly three decades. The Bretton Woods system eventually <select class="pte-select" data-answer="collapsed"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="collapsing">collapsing</option><option value="collapses">collapses</option><option value="collapse">collapse</option><option value="collapsed">collapsed</option></select> in 1971 when the United States ended the dollar\'s convertibility to gold.',
    'In 1913, Henry Ford introduced the moving assembly line to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="accelerate"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="accelerates">accelerates</option><option value="accelerate">accelerate</option><option value="accelerating">accelerating</option><option value="accelerated">accelerated</option></select> the production of automobiles. This innovation allowed workers to remain in one place while parts <select class="pte-select" data-answer="moved"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="move">move</option><option value="moves">moves</option><option value="moved">moved</option><option value="moving">moving</option></select> past them on a conveyor belt. As a result, the time needed to build a Model T became dramatically more <select class="pte-select" data-answer="efficient"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="wasteful">wasteful</option><option value="efficient">efficient</option><option value="sporadic">sporadic</option><option value="extravagant">extravagant</option></select>. Ford\'s methods soon became remarkably <select class="pte-select" data-answer="widespread"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="rare">rare</option><option value="temporary">temporary</option><option value="widespread">widespread</option><option value="exclusive">exclusive</option></select> across the manufacturing world, influencing industries far beyond automobiles.',
    'The Great Depression began after the U.S. stock market <select class="pte-select" data-answer="crashed"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="crashes">crashes</option><option value="crashed">crashed</option><option value="crashing">crashing</option><option value="crash">crash</option></select> in October 1929. Banks failed, unemployment soared, and consumer spending became severely <select class="pte-select" data-answer="restricted"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="restricted">restricted</option><option value="generous">generous</option><option value="flexible">flexible</option><option value="abundant">abundant</option></select>. President Franklin D. Roosevelt\'s New Deal aimed to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="stimulate"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="stimulates">stimulates</option><option value="stimulate">stimulate</option><option value="stimulated">stimulated</option><option value="stimulating">stimulating</option></select> economic recovery through public works programs. By the early 1940s, wartime production had made many of the economic hardships of the previous decade far less <select class="pte-select" data-answer="severe"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="predictable">predictable</option><option value="advantageous">advantageous</option><option value="severe">severe</option><option value="temporary">temporary</option></select>.',
    'David Ricardo\'s theory of comparative advantage explains why nations <select class="pte-select" data-answer="benefit"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="benefit">benefit</option><option value="benefiting">benefiting</option><option value="benefits">benefits</option><option value="benefited">benefited</option></select> from specializing in certain goods. According to this theory, a country should produce goods for which it has the <select class="pte-select" data-answer="lowest"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="steepest">steepest</option><option value="lowest">lowest</option><option value="broadest">broadest</option><option value="highest">highest</option></select> opportunity cost. Trade between nations can then leave both parties economically <select class="pte-select" data-answer="stronger"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="weaker">weaker</option><option value="poorer">poorer</option><option value="stronger">stronger</option><option value="worse">worse</option></select>, even if one country is more efficient at producing everything. This principle continues to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="underpin"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="underpin">underpin</option><option value="underpins">underpins</option><option value="underpinning">underpinning</option><option value="underpinned">underpinned</option></select> modern arguments in favor of free trade agreements.',
    'The evolution of marine biology has fundamentally changed how we understand the solar system. Historically, researchers believed that DNA <select class="pte-select" data-answer="played"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="plays">plays</option><option value="playing">playing</option><option value="played">played</option><option value="play">play</option></select> a minor role. However, recent studies have demonstrated that it is <select class="pte-select" data-answer="crucial"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="trivial">trivial</option><option value="crucial">crucial</option><option value="optional">optional</option><option value="irrelevant">irrelevant</option></select> to the overall system. As we continue to explore this fascinating field, it becomes <select class="pte-select" data-answer="evident"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="evident">evident</option><option value="obscure">obscure</option><option value="doubtful">doubtful</option><option value="hidden">hidden</option></select> that our previous models were incomplete. Future breakthroughs will likely <select class="pte-select" data-answer="rely"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="rely">rely</option><option value="relying">relying</option><option value="relied">relied</option><option value="relies">relies</option></select> heavily on interdisciplinary collaboration.',
    'Bitcoin, launched in 2009 by the pseudonymous Satoshi Nakamoto, was designed to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="create"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="create">create</option><option value="creating">creating</option><option value="created">created</option><option value="creates">creates</option></select> a decentralized digital currency. Transactions are recorded on a public ledger called a blockchain, which makes them difficult to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="alter"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="alter">alter</option><option value="altering">altering</option><option value="altered">altered</option><option value="alters">alters</option></select>. Supporters argue that cryptocurrencies offer a <select class="pte-select" data-answer="viable"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="fragile">fragile</option><option value="redundant">redundant</option><option value="viable">viable</option><option value="obsolete">obsolete</option></select> alternative to traditional banking systems. Critics, however, point to the extreme price <select class="pte-select" data-answer="volatility"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="permanence">permanence</option><option value="stability">stability</option><option value="volatility">volatility</option><option value="uniformity">uniformity</option></select> of Bitcoin as a major obstacle to its everyday use.',
    'Founded in 1602, the Dutch East India Company is widely considered the first company to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="issue"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="issued">issued</option><option value="issues">issues</option><option value="issuing">issuing</option><option value="issue">issue</option></select> shares to the public. This innovation allowed the company to accumulate enormous <select class="pte-select" data-answer="capital"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="inventory">inventory</option><option value="personnel">personnel</option><option value="capital">capital</option><option value="debt">debt</option></select> for its trading voyages to Asia. Investors could buy and sell shares on the Amsterdam Stock Exchange, which became remarkably <select class="pte-select" data-answer="liquid"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="scarce">scarce</option><option value="static">static</option><option value="exclusive">exclusive</option><option value="liquid">liquid</option></select> for its time. The company\'s structure <select class="pte-select" data-answer="served"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="serving">serving</option><option value="served">served</option><option value="serves">serves</option><option value="serve">serve</option></select> as a model for the modern corporation that emerged in later centuries.',
    'Keynes argued that a powerful fiscal <select class="pte-select" data-answer="stimulus"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="austerity">austerity</option><option value="stimulus">stimulus</option><option value="contraction">contraction</option><option value="surplus">surplus</option></select> could lift an economy out of a deep recession. During downturns, Keynes believed that private investment often becomes too <select class="pte-select" data-answer="weak"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="constant">constant</option><option value="weak">weak</option><option value="excessive">excessive</option><option value="robust">robust</option></select> to sustain full employment. He therefore recommended that governments <select class="pte-select" data-answer="adopt"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="adopts">adopts</option><option value="adopted">adopted</option><option value="adopt">adopt</option><option value="adopting">adopting</option></select> deficit spending during economic slumps. Keynesian policies <select class="pte-select" data-answer="became"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="became">became</option><option value="becoming">becoming</option><option value="become">become</option><option value="becomes">becomes</option></select> widely influential after World War II, shaping the fiscal policies of many nations.',
