    'Under the gold standard, a country\'s currency was directly <select class="pte-select" data-answer="linked"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="link">link</option><option value="linked">linked</option><option value="links">links</option><option value="linking">linking</option></select> to a specific quantity of gold. This system limited how much money governments could print, since currency <select class="pte-select" data-answer="issuance"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="circulation">circulation</option><option value="taxation">taxation</option><option value="expenditure">expenditure</option><option value="issuance">issuance</option></select> was tied to gold reserves. Many economists argue that the gold standard made economies less <select class="pte-select" data-answer="flexible"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="prosperous">prosperous</option><option value="efficient">efficient</option><option value="competitive">competitive</option><option value="flexible">flexible</option></select> in responding to financial crises. Most major economies <select class="pte-select" data-answer="abandoned"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="abandoned">abandoned</option><option value="abandons">abandons</option><option value="abandoning">abandoning</option><option value="abandon">abandon</option></select> the gold standard by the mid-20th century in favor of fiat currencies.',
