    'Ray Kroc <select class="pte-select" data-answer="built"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="build">build</option><option value="building">building</option><option value="builds">builds</option><option value="built">built</option></select> the McDonald\'s franchising system in the 1950s, transforming a single restaurant into a global chain. Under this model, independent franchisees provide an upfront <select class="pte-select" data-answer="fee"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="donation">donation</option><option value="discount">discount</option><option value="fee">fee</option><option value="deposit">deposit</option></select> and ongoing royalties in exchange for using the company\'s brand and systems. This approach allowed McDonald\'s to expand rapidly while keeping capital investment relatively <select class="pte-select" data-answer="low"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="low">low</option><option value="mandatory">mandatory</option><option value="unpredictable">unpredictable</option><option value="excessive">excessive</option></select> for the parent company. Today, franchising <select class="pte-select" data-answer="remains"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="remains">remains</option><option value="remain">remain</option><option value="remaining">remaining</option><option value="remained">remained</option></select> a common strategy for businesses seeking rapid growth with limited direct investment.',
    'Before the invention of currency, early societies commonly <select class="pte-select" data-answer="exchanged"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="exchanges">exchanges</option><option value="exchanged">exchanged</option><option value="exchanging">exchanging</option><option value="exchange">exchange</option></select> goods and services directly through barter. Barter systems were often <select class="pte-select" data-answer="inefficient"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="flawless">flawless</option><option value="permanent">permanent</option><option value="inefficient">inefficient</option><option value="popular">popular</option></select> because they required a rare "double coincidence of wants" between two parties. To solve this problem, many cultures began to rely on precious metals such as gold and silver as a medium of <select class="pte-select" data-answer="exchange"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="taxation">taxation</option><option value="storage">storage</option><option value="exchange">exchange</option><option value="production">production</option></select>. Eventually, standardized coins and later paper money <select class="pte-select" data-answer="replaced"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="replaces">replaces</option><option value="replace">replace</option><option value="replacing">replacing</option><option value="replaced">replaced</option></select> these metals as the dominant forms of currency worldwide.',
