    'At the height of summer the Greenland, tourist ships move gently around the coast. Even 20 years ago such sights would have been unthinkable, but today people are willing to pay large sums of money to see the last real wilderness in the world. In the Ice Caps, careless human exploitation <select class="pte-select" data-answer="has damaged"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="have damaged">have damaged</option><option value="has damaged">has damaged</option><option value="damaging">damaging</option><option value="damage">damage</option></select> the fragile ecosystem. Today concerned governments are trying to find ways to develop the region <select class="pte-select" data-answer="in the future"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="in the future">in the future</option><option value="ever before">ever before</option><option value="in the past">in the past</option><option value="on one side">on one side</option></select> special natural environment. <select class="pte-select" data-answer="Although"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="Because">Because</option><option value="Since">Since</option><option value="Although">Although</option><option value="When">When</option></select> the Greenland is less accessible than the Ice Caps, it is still largely undamaged by humans.',
    'During the early Renaissance, Filippo Brunelleschi <select class="pte-select" data-answer="developed"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="develop">develop</option><option value="develops">develops</option><option value="developed">developed</option><option value="developing">developing</option></select> the mathematical principles of linear perspective through architectural experiments in Florence. His discoveries enabled painters to create a <select class="pte-select" data-answer="convincing"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="symbolic">symbolic</option><option value="decorative">decorative</option><option value="flattened">flattened</option><option value="convincing">convincing</option></select> illusion of three-dimensional space on a flat surface. The architect and theorist Leon Battista Alberti later <select class="pte-select" data-answer="codified"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="abandoned">abandoned</option><option value="discovered">discovered</option><option value="codified">codified</option><option value="concealed">concealed</option></select> these principles in his treatise Della Pittura, published in 1435. Since then, linear perspective <select class="pte-select" data-answer="has remained"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="remain">remain</option><option value="have remained">have remained</option><option value="remaining">remaining</option><option value="has remained">has remained</option></select> a foundational technique in Western representational art.',
    'In the buon fresco technique, artists apply pigment directly onto <select class="pte-select" data-answer="wet"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="hardened">hardened</option><option value="wet">wet</option><option value="dry">dry</option><option value="painted">painted</option></select> plaster so that the color bonds permanently with the wall as it dries. Michelangelo famously <select class="pte-select" data-answer="employed"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="employs">employs</option><option value="employing">employing</option><option value="employ">employ</option><option value="employed">employed</option></select> this demanding method to complete the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel between 1508 and 1512. Because the plaster set within hours, painters divided each composition into daily <select class="pte-select" data-answer="sections"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="sections">sections</option><option value="layers">layers</option><option value="borders">borders</option><option value="frames">frames</option></select> known as giornate. Today, conservators <select class="pte-select" data-answer="preserve"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="preserve">preserve</option><option value="preserved">preserved</option><option value="preserves">preserves</option><option value="preserving">preserving</option></select> these fresco cycles using careful cleaning and minimal intervention.',
    'In the 1870s, a group of Parisian painters <select class="pte-select" data-answer="rebelled"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="rebelling">rebelling</option><option value="rebels">rebels</option><option value="rebelled">rebelled</option><option value="rebel">rebel</option></select> against the rigid conventions of the official Salon exhibitions. Artists such as Claude Monet preferred to paint outdoors, capturing the <select class="pte-select" data-answer="fleeting"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="permanent">permanent</option><option value="artificial">artificial</option><option value="fleeting">fleeting</option><option value="muted">muted</option></select> effects of natural light at different times of day. Their visible, broken brushstrokes were widely considered <select class="pte-select" data-answer="unfinished"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="meticulous">meticulous</option><option value="flawless">flawless</option><option value="traditional">traditional</option><option value="unfinished">unfinished</option></select> by conservative critics of the time. Despite early ridicule, Impressionism eventually <select class="pte-select" data-answer="became"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="becoming">becoming</option><option value="became">became</option><option value="become">become</option><option value="becomes">becomes</option></select> one of the most celebrated movements in art history.',
    'Around 1907, Pablo Picasso and Georges Braque <select class="pte-select" data-answer="developed"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="developed">developed</option><option value="develop">develop</option><option value="develops">develops</option><option value="developing">developing</option></select> a radical new style that fragmented objects into geometric planes. Cubist paintings often depict a single subject from multiple <select class="pte-select" data-answer="angles"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="shadows">shadows</option><option value="textures">textures</option><option value="colors">colors</option><option value="angles">angles</option></select> simultaneously, rather than a single fixed viewpoint. This approach challenged the long-standing assumption that a painting should offer a <select class="pte-select" data-answer="singular"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="colorful">colorful</option><option value="distorted">distorted</option><option value="singular">singular</option><option value="emotional">emotional</option></select> view of reality. Analytic Cubism later <select class="pte-select" data-answer="evolved"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="evolved">evolved</option><option value="evolve">evolve</option><option value="evolves">evolves</option><option value="evolving">evolving</option></select> into Synthetic Cubism, which incorporated collage elements such as newspaper clippings.',
    'The evolution of cognitive psychology has fundamentally changed how we understand the oceanic ecosystem. Historically, researchers believed that temperature <select class="pte-select" data-answer="played"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="plays">plays</option><option value="playing">playing</option><option value="played">played</option><option value="play">play</option></select> a minor role. However, recent studies have demonstrated that it is <select class="pte-select" data-answer="crucial"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="trivial">trivial</option><option value="crucial">crucial</option><option value="optional">optional</option><option value="irrelevant">irrelevant</option></select> to the overall system. As we continue to explore this fascinating field, it becomes <select class="pte-select" data-answer="evident"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="evident">evident</option><option value="obscure">obscure</option><option value="doubtful">doubtful</option><option value="hidden">hidden</option></select> that our previous models were incomplete. Future breakthroughs will likely <select class="pte-select" data-answer="rely"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="rely">rely</option><option value="relying">relying</option><option value="relied">relied</option><option value="relies">relies</option></select> heavily on interdisciplinary collaboration.',
    'Gothic cathedrals of the twelfth and thirteenth centuries introduced pointed arches, ribbed vaults, and flying buttresses that allowed walls to reach unprecedented <select class="pte-select" data-answer="heights"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="depths">depths</option><option value="thicknesses">thicknesses</option><option value="heights">heights</option><option value="widths">widths</option></select>. Flying buttresses <select class="pte-select" data-answer="transfer"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="transferred">transferred</option><option value="transfers">transfers</option><option value="transferring">transferring</option><option value="transfer">transfer</option></select> the outward thrust of the stone vaults away from the walls and down to the ground. This innovation freed up wall space for large stained-glass windows that filled interiors with <select class="pte-select" data-answer="vibrant"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="faint">faint</option><option value="vibrant">vibrant</option><option value="monochrome">monochrome</option><option value="artificial">artificial</option></select> colored light. Chartres Cathedral, largely <select class="pte-select" data-answer="rebuilt"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="rebuild">rebuild</option><option value="rebuilt">rebuilt</option><option value="rebuilds">rebuilds</option><option value="rebuilding">rebuilding</option></select> between 1194 and 1220, remains one of the finest surviving examples of French Gothic architecture.',
    'In the late sixteenth century, Caravaggio <select class="pte-select" data-answer="employed"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="employed">employed</option><option value="employs">employs</option><option value="employ">employ</option><option value="employing">employing</option></select> dramatic contrasts of light and shadow to heighten the emotional intensity of his paintings. This technique, known as chiaroscuro, uses a single strong light source to create a <select class="pte-select" data-answer="heightened"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="muted">muted</option><option value="heightened">heightened</option><option value="diminished">diminished</option><option value="flattened">flattened</option></select> sense of depth and drama. Caravaggio often depicted biblical scenes with ordinary, even <select class="pte-select" data-answer="unrefined"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="idealized">idealized</option><option value="angelic">angelic</option><option value="unrefined">unrefined</option><option value="regal">regal</option></select> models, which shocked some of his patrons. His innovations subsequently <select class="pte-select" data-answer="influenced"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="influenced">influenced</option><option value="influences">influences</option><option value="influencing">influencing</option><option value="influence">influence</option></select> generations of Baroque painters across Europe, including Rembrandt and Artemisia Gentileschi.',
    'Classical Greek sculptors introduced a naturalistic stance in which a figure\'s weight rests <select class="pte-select" data-answer="primarily"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="symbolically">symbolically</option><option value="equally">equally</option><option value="rarely">rarely</option><option value="primarily">primarily</option></select> on one leg, creating a subtle shift in the hips and shoulders. This technique, called contrapposto, <select class="pte-select" data-answer="makes"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="made">made</option><option value="making">making</option><option value="make">make</option><option value="makes">makes</option></select> statues appear more relaxed and lifelike than earlier rigid poses. The sculptor Polykleitos even wrote a treatise, the Kanon, outlining <select class="pte-select" data-answer="mathematical"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="mathematical">mathematical</option><option value="emotional">emotional</option><option value="random">random</option><option value="decorative">decorative</option></select> proportions he considered ideal for the human body. These principles <select class="pte-select" data-answer="exerted"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="exerted">exerted</option><option value="exerts">exerts</option><option value="exerting">exerting</option><option value="exert">exert</option></select> enormous influence on later Roman copies and Renaissance sculpture alike.',
    'Byzantine artisans <select class="pte-select" data-answer="used"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="using">using</option><option value="use">use</option><option value="used">used</option><option value="uses">uses</option></select> small pieces of colored glass and gold-backed tesserae to construct elaborate wall and ceiling mosaics. The gold tesserae were set at slightly <select class="pte-select" data-answer="irregular"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="vertical">vertical</option><option value="irregular">irregular</option><option value="uniform">uniform</option><option value="polished">polished</option></select> angles so that they would catch and reflect flickering candlelight. Figures in these mosaics were typically rendered in a flat, <select class="pte-select" data-answer="stylized"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="proportional">proportional</option><option value="stylized">stylized</option><option value="photographic">photographic</option><option value="spontaneous">spontaneous</option></select> style rather than with naturalistic perspective. The mosaics of the Hagia Sophia and the churches of Ravenna still <select class="pte-select" data-answer="captivate"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="captivating">captivating</option><option value="captivate">captivate</option><option value="captivates">captivates</option><option value="captivated">captivated</option></select> visitors with their shimmering, otherworldly quality.',
    'During the seventeenth century, Dutch painters <select class="pte-select" data-answer="favored"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="favored">favored</option><option value="favor">favor</option><option value="favoring">favoring</option><option value="favors">favors</option></select> everyday domestic scenes rather than religious or mythological subjects. Johannes Vermeer was especially skilled at rendering the <select class="pte-select" data-answer="subtle"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="artificial">artificial</option><option value="fading">fading</option><option value="subtle">subtle</option><option value="harsh">harsh</option></select> quality of light falling through a window. His pigments, including the costly ultramarine derived from lapis lazuli, gave his paintings a distinctive <select class="pte-select" data-answer="luminous"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="faded">faded</option><option value="luminous">luminous</option><option value="murky">murky</option><option value="harsh">harsh</option></select> tone. Only a small number of Vermeer\'s paintings <select class="pte-select" data-answer="survive"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="survives">survives</option><option value="surviving">surviving</option><option value="survive">survive</option><option value="survived">survived</option></select> today, making each one highly prized.',
    'In the 1920s, Surrealist artists <select class="pte-select" data-answer="sought"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="sought">sought</option><option value="seeking">seeking</option><option value="seeks">seeks</option><option value="seek">seek</option></select> to access the unconscious mind through dreamlike, irrational imagery. Salvador Dalí\'s melting clocks in The Persistence of Memory suggest that time itself is <select class="pte-select" data-answer="fluid"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="eternal">eternal</option><option value="fluid">fluid</option><option value="precise">precise</option><option value="mechanical">mechanical</option></select> rather than fixed. Many Surrealists practiced automatic drawing, allowing images to emerge without <select class="pte-select" data-answer="deliberate"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="deliberate">deliberate</option><option value="external">external</option><option value="physical">physical</option><option value="artistic">artistic</option></select> control from the conscious mind. The movement eventually <select class="pte-select" data-answer="extended"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="extend">extend</option><option value="extending">extending</option><option value="extends">extends</option><option value="extended">extended</option></select> across Europe and the Americas, influencing film, literature, and photography.',
    'The evolution of astrophysics has fundamentally changed how we understand the solar system. Historically, researchers believed that temperature <select class="pte-select" data-answer="played"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="plays">plays</option><option value="playing">playing</option><option value="played">played</option><option value="play">play</option></select> a minor role. However, recent studies have demonstrated that it is <select class="pte-select" data-answer="crucial"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="trivial">trivial</option><option value="crucial">crucial</option><option value="optional">optional</option><option value="irrelevant">irrelevant</option></select> to the overall system. As we continue to explore this fascinating field, it becomes <select class="pte-select" data-answer="evident"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="evident">evident</option><option value="obscure">obscure</option><option value="doubtful">doubtful</option><option value="hidden">hidden</option></select> that our previous models were incomplete. Future breakthroughs will likely <select class="pte-select" data-answer="rely"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="rely">rely</option><option value="relying">relying</option><option value="relied">relied</option><option value="relies">relies</option></select> heavily on interdisciplinary collaboration.',
    'After the Second World War, New York <select class="pte-select" data-answer="replaced"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="replaced">replaced</option><option value="replacing">replacing</option><option value="replace">replace</option><option value="replaces">replaces</option></select> Paris as the leading center of the international art world. Jackson Pollock developed a <select class="pte-select" data-answer="spontaneous"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="geometric">geometric</option><option value="meticulous">meticulous</option><option value="conventional">conventional</option><option value="spontaneous">spontaneous</option></select> technique of dripping and flinging paint onto canvases laid on the floor. Critics argued that these paintings expressed raw emotion rather than any <select class="pte-select" data-answer="recognizable"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="hidden">hidden</option><option value="colorful">colorful</option><option value="recognizable">recognizable</option><option value="abstract">abstract</option></select> subject matter. Abstract Expressionism subsequently <select class="pte-select" data-answer="pushed"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="pushed">pushed</option><option value="pushes">pushes</option><option value="pushing">pushing</option><option value="push">push</option></select> younger artists toward increasingly minimal and conceptual forms of art.',
    'The evolution of genetics has fundamentally changed how we understand the solar system. Historically, researchers believed that DNA <select class="pte-select" data-answer="played"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="plays">plays</option><option value="playing">playing</option><option value="played">played</option><option value="play">play</option></select> a minor role. However, recent studies have demonstrated that it is <select class="pte-select" data-answer="crucial"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="trivial">trivial</option><option value="crucial">crucial</option><option value="optional">optional</option><option value="irrelevant">irrelevant</option></select> to the overall system. As we continue to explore this fascinating field, it becomes <select class="pte-select" data-answer="evident"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="evident">evident</option><option value="obscure">obscure</option><option value="doubtful">doubtful</option><option value="hidden">hidden</option></select> that our previous models were incomplete. Future breakthroughs will likely <select class="pte-select" data-answer="rely"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="rely">rely</option><option value="relying">relying</option><option value="relied">relied</option><option value="relies">relies</option></select> heavily on interdisciplinary collaboration.',
    'In the late eighteenth century, Jacques-Louis David <select class="pte-select" data-answer="revived"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="reviving">reviving</option><option value="revive">revive</option><option value="revived">revived</option><option value="revives">revives</option></select> classical antiquity as a moral and political model through his paintings. Neoclassical works favored clear outlines, balanced composition, and <select class="pte-select" data-answer="sober"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="chaotic">chaotic</option><option value="ornamental">ornamental</option><option value="playful">playful</option><option value="sober">sober</option></select> restraint over the ornate excess of the preceding Rococo style. Subjects were often drawn from Roman history to illustrate <select class="pte-select" data-answer="noble"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="trivial">trivial</option><option value="private">private</option><option value="comedic">comedic</option><option value="noble">noble</option></select> virtues such as civic duty and self-sacrifice. David\'s Oath of the Horatii <select class="pte-select" data-answer="received"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="receiving">receiving</option><option value="receive">receive</option><option value="received">received</option><option value="receives">receives</option></select> widespread acclaim when it was exhibited in 1785.',
    'In contrast to Neoclassical restraint, Romantic painters <select class="pte-select" data-answer="emphasized"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="emphasized">emphasized</option><option value="emphasizes">emphasizes</option><option value="emphasize">emphasize</option><option value="emphasizing">emphasizing</option></select> intense emotion, imagination, and the power of nature. J.M.W. Turner\'s turbulent seascapes convey a sense of the <select class="pte-select" data-answer="sublime"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="sublime">sublime</option><option value="mundane">mundane</option><option value="ordinary">ordinary</option><option value="predictable">predictable</option></select>, an overwhelming feeling inspired by nature\'s raw power. His loose brushwork and swirling color dissolved forms into near-<select class="pte-select" data-answer="abstract"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="photographic">photographic</option><option value="abstract">abstract</option><option value="linear">linear</option><option value="symmetrical">symmetrical</option></select> compositions decades before abstraction emerged. Turner\'s later works increasingly <select class="pte-select" data-answer="abandoned"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="abandoning">abandoning</option><option value="abandon">abandon</option><option value="abandoned">abandoned</option><option value="abandons">abandons</option></select> recognizable detail in favor of atmosphere and light.',
    'At the height of summer the Arctic, tourist ships move gently around the coast. Even 30 years ago such sights would have been unthinkable, but today people are willing to pay large sums of money to see the last real wilderness in the world. In the Northern Hemisphere, careless human exploitation <select class="pte-select" data-answer="has damaged"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="have damaged">have damaged</option><option value="has damaged">has damaged</option><option value="damaging">damaging</option><option value="damage">damage</option></select> the fragile ecosystem. Today concerned governments are trying to find ways to develop the region <select class="pte-select" data-answer="in the future"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="in the future">in the future</option><option value="ever before">ever before</option><option value="in the past">in the past</option><option value="on one side">on one side</option></select> special natural environment. <select class="pte-select" data-answer="Although"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="Because">Because</option><option value="Since">Since</option><option value="Although">Although</option><option value="When">When</option></select> the Arctic is less accessible than the Northern Hemisphere, it is still largely undamaged by humans.',
    'At the turn of the twentieth century, Art Nouveau designers <select class="pte-select" data-answer="adopted"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="adopt">adopt</option><option value="adopted">adopted</option><option value="adopts">adopts</option><option value="adopting">adopting</option></select> flowing, organic lines inspired by plants and natural forms. Alphonse Mucha\'s posters featured women with long, <select class="pte-select" data-answer="sinuous"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="sinuous">sinuous</option><option value="coarse">coarse</option><option value="cropped">cropped</option><option value="straight">straight</option></select> hair intertwined with floral and vine motifs. The style rejected the rigid historicism of earlier academic art in favor of <select class="pte-select" data-answer="decorative"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="geometric">geometric</option><option value="industrial">industrial</option><option value="decorative">decorative</option><option value="monochrome">monochrome</option></select> ornament drawn from nature. Art Nouveau eventually <select class="pte-select" data-answer="extended"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="extended">extended</option><option value="extending">extending</option><option value="extends">extends</option><option value="extend">extend</option></select> across architecture, furniture, jewelry, and graphic design throughout Europe.',
    'In the 1960s, Andy Warhol <select class="pte-select" data-answer="elevated"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="elevating">elevating</option><option value="elevates">elevates</option><option value="elevate">elevate</option><option value="elevated">elevated</option></select> everyday consumer products and celebrity images as subjects worthy of fine art. His silkscreen prints of Campbell\'s Soup cans blurred the line between <select class="pte-select" data-answer="commercial"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="spiritual">spiritual</option><option value="ancient">ancient</option><option value="private">private</option><option value="commercial">commercial</option></select> culture and high art. By repeating the same image with slight variations, Warhol commented on the <select class="pte-select" data-answer="repetitive"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="repetitive">repetitive</option><option value="unpredictable">unpredictable</option><option value="unique">unique</option><option value="handmade">handmade</option></select> nature of mass production. Pop Art subsequently <select class="pte-select" data-answer="encouraged"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="encourage">encourage</option><option value="encouraged">encouraged</option><option value="encouraging">encouraging</option><option value="encourages">encourages</option></select> artists to question the boundary between advertising and artistic expression.',
    'Sculptors have long relied on the lost-wax method, or cire perdue, to cast <select class="pte-select" data-answer="intricate"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="intricate">intricate</option><option value="blurred">blurred</option><option value="flat">flat</option><option value="simplified">simplified</option></select> bronze figures with fine detail. The process begins with a detailed wax model that is later <select class="pte-select" data-answer="replaced"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="replace">replace</option><option value="replacing">replacing</option><option value="replaced">replaced</option><option value="replaces">replaces</option></select> by molten bronze poured into a mold. Because the wax model melts away during casting, each lost-wax bronze is essentially a <select class="pte-select" data-answer="unique"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="temporary">temporary</option><option value="unique">unique</option><option value="flawed">flawed</option><option value="symbolic">symbolic</option></select> original that cannot be exactly duplicated. This ancient technique still <select class="pte-select" data-answer="sees"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="sees">sees</option><option value="seen">seen</option><option value="seeing">seeing</option><option value="see">see</option></select> widespread use among sculptors and foundries today.',
    'Ancient Egyptian artists <select class="pte-select" data-answer="depicted"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="depicts">depicts</option><option value="depicting">depicting</option><option value="depict">depict</option><option value="depicted">depicted</option></select> the human figure using a distinctive composite perspective, combining frontal and profile views. This convention shows the head, legs, and feet in profile while the eye and torso appear <select class="pte-select" data-answer="fully"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="barely">barely</option><option value="fully">fully</option><option value="rarely">rarely</option><option value="occasionally">occasionally</option></select> facing forward. Figures of greater social importance, such as pharaohs, were often painted <select class="pte-select" data-answer="larger"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="smaller">smaller</option><option value="dimmer">dimmer</option><option value="flatter">flatter</option><option value="larger">larger</option></select> than servants or lesser figures. These artistic conventions <select class="pte-select" data-answer="remained"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="remaining">remaining</option><option value="remain">remain</option><option value="remained">remained</option><option value="remains">remains</option></select> remarkably consistent for over three thousand years of Egyptian history.',
    'At the height of summer the Greenland, tourist ships move gently around the coast. Even 100 years ago such sights would have been unthinkable, but today people are willing to pay large sums of money to see the last real wilderness in the world. In the Tundra, careless human exploitation <select class="pte-select" data-answer="has damaged"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="have damaged">have damaged</option><option value="has damaged">has damaged</option><option value="damaging">damaging</option><option value="damage">damage</option></select> the fragile ecosystem. Today concerned governments are trying to find ways to develop the region <select class="pte-select" data-answer="in the future"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="in the future">in the future</option><option value="ever before">ever before</option><option value="in the past">in the past</option><option value="on one side">on one side</option></select> special natural environment. <select class="pte-select" data-answer="Although"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="Because">Because</option><option value="Since">Since</option><option value="Although">Although</option><option value="When">When</option></select> the Greenland is less accessible than the Tundra, it is still largely undamaged by humans.',
    'In eighteenth-century France, Rococo artists <select class="pte-select" data-answer="rejected"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="rejected">rejected</option><option value="rejects">rejects</option><option value="reject">reject</option><option value="rejecting">rejecting</option></select> the grandeur of Baroque art in favor of lighter, more playful themes. Jean-Honoré Fragonard\'s The Swing depicts a scene of flirtation rendered in soft, <select class="pte-select" data-answer="delicate"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="industrial">industrial</option><option value="dull">dull</option><option value="harsh">harsh</option><option value="delicate">delicate</option></select> pastel colors. Rococo interiors favored asymmetrical curves, gilded scrollwork, and an overall sense of <select class="pte-select" data-answer="playful"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="military">military</option><option value="somber">somber</option><option value="playful">playful</option><option value="austere">austere</option></select> elegance. The style eventually <select class="pte-select" data-answer="attracted"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="attracted">attracted</option><option value="attracting">attracting</option><option value="attract">attract</option><option value="attracts">attracts</option></select> criticism for its perceived frivolity as Neoclassicism rose in popularity.',
    'In the 1960s, Minimalist artists such as Donald Judd <select class="pte-select" data-answer="eliminated"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="eliminated">eliminated</option><option value="eliminating">eliminating</option><option value="eliminate">eliminate</option><option value="eliminates">eliminates</option></select> ornamentation and personal expression from their sculptural work. Judd\'s box-like forms, often fabricated from industrial materials, emphasized <select class="pte-select" data-answer="precise"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="precise">precise</option><option value="organic">organic</option><option value="emotional">emotional</option><option value="chaotic">chaotic</option></select> geometry over emotional or symbolic content. Minimalist works were meant to be experienced as literal objects rather than as <select class="pte-select" data-answer="symbolic"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="industrial">industrial</option><option value="symbolic">symbolic</option><option value="physical">physical</option><option value="structural">structural</option></select> representations of something else. This emphasis on simplicity and repetition later <select class="pte-select" data-answer="shaped"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="shaped">shaped</option><option value="shape">shape</option><option value="shaping">shaping</option><option value="shapes">shapes</option></select> conceptual and installation art of subsequent decades.',
    'Museum conservators <select class="pte-select" data-answer="conduct"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="conducting">conducting</option><option value="conduct">conduct</option><option value="conducts">conducts</option><option value="conducted">conducted</option></select> scientific analysis, including infrared imaging and pigment testing, before undertaking any restoration work. Modern conservation ethics favor <select class="pte-select" data-answer="minimal"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="minimal">minimal</option><option value="cosmetic">cosmetic</option><option value="permanent">permanent</option><option value="aggressive">aggressive</option></select> intervention, preserving as much original material as possible. Restorers favor reversible materials so that any later additions can be distinguished from the <select class="pte-select" data-answer="original"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="original">original</option><option value="modern">modern</option><option value="hidden">hidden</option><option value="damaged">damaged</option></select> artwork by experts. Ongoing conservation efforts <select class="pte-select" data-answer="ensure"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="ensures">ensures</option><option value="ensure">ensure</option><option value="ensuring">ensuring</option><option value="ensured">ensured</option></select> that masterpieces such as the Sistine Chapel ceiling remain visible for future generations.',
    'At the height of summer the Galapagos, tourist ships move gently around the coast. Even 30 years ago such sights would have been unthinkable, but today people are willing to pay large sums of money to see the last real wilderness in the world. In the Northern Hemisphere, careless human exploitation <select class="pte-select" data-answer="has damaged"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="have damaged">have damaged</option><option value="has damaged">has damaged</option><option value="damaging">damaging</option><option value="damage">damage</option></select> the fragile ecosystem. Today concerned governments are trying to find ways to develop the region <select class="pte-select" data-answer="in the future"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="in the future">in the future</option><option value="ever before">ever before</option><option value="in the past">in the past</option><option value="on one side">on one side</option></select> special natural environment. <select class="pte-select" data-answer="Although"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="Because">Because</option><option value="Since">Since</option><option value="Although">Although</option><option value="When">When</option></select> the Galapagos is less accessible than the Northern Hemisphere, it is still largely undamaged by humans.',
    'The invention of the printing press by Johannes Gutenberg in the fifteenth century <select class="pte-select" data-answer="transformed"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="transforms">transforms</option><option value="transforming">transforming</option><option value="transformed">transformed</option><option value="transform">transform</option></select> the way books were produced across Europe. Before this innovation, manuscripts had to be copied by hand, a process that was famously <select class="pte-select" data-answer="laborious"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="convenient">convenient</option><option value="swift">swift</option><option value="effortless">effortless</option><option value="laborious">laborious</option></select> and expensive. Gutenberg\'s use of movable metal type made it possible to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="reproduce"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="reproduces">reproduces</option><option value="reproduce">reproduce</option><option value="reproduced">reproduced</option><option value="reproducing">reproducing</option></select> multiple copies of a single text quickly. As a result, printed works became increasingly <select class="pte-select" data-answer="widespread"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="temporary">temporary</option><option value="forbidden">forbidden</option><option value="widespread">widespread</option><option value="scarce">scarce</option></select> throughout European society.',
    'William Shakespeare is widely credited with having <select class="pte-select" data-answer="introduced"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="introduced">introduced</option><option value="introduces">introduces</option><option value="introducing">introducing</option><option value="introduce">introduce</option></select> hundreds of new words into the English language. Many of these coinages, such as \'eyeball\' and \'lonely,\' remain <select class="pte-select" data-answer="common"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="incomprehensible">incomprehensible</option><option value="forbidden">forbidden</option><option value="common">common</option><option value="obsolete">obsolete</option></select> in everyday speech today. Scholars continue to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="examine"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="examined">examined</option><option value="examines">examines</option><option value="examine">examine</option><option value="examining">examining</option></select> his plays for evidence of linguistic invention and wordplay. His inventive use of language has left a <select class="pte-select" data-answer="lasting"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="invisible">invisible</option><option value="lasting">lasting</option><option value="negligible">negligible</option><option value="temporary">temporary</option></select> mark on modern English vocabulary.',
    'In the early twentieth century, writers such as Virginia Woolf <select class="pte-select" data-answer="developed"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="develops">develops</option><option value="develop">develop</option><option value="developing">developing</option><option value="developed">developed</option></select> a narrative technique known as stream of consciousness. This technique attempts to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="capture"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="capture">capture</option><option value="captures">captures</option><option value="captured">captured</option><option value="capturing">capturing</option></select> the continuous flow of a character\'s thoughts and impressions. Unlike traditional narration, stream of consciousness often appears <select class="pte-select" data-answer="fragmented"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="predictable">predictable</option><option value="formal">formal</option><option value="fragmented">fragmented</option><option value="polished">polished</option></select> and lacking clear punctuation. Novels like \'Mrs Dalloway\' remain <select class="pte-select" data-answer="influential"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="conventional">conventional</option><option value="influential">influential</option><option value="forgettable">forgettable</option><option value="irrelevant">irrelevant</option></select> examples of this modernist experimentation.',
    'Between the fifteenth and seventeenth centuries, English pronunciation <select class="pte-select" data-answer="changed"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="changing">changing</option><option value="change">change</option><option value="changed">changed</option><option value="changes">changes</option></select> dramatically in a phenomenon known as the Great Vowel Shift. During this period, long vowel sounds began to <select class="pte-select" data-answer="become"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="becoming">becoming</option><option value="becomes">becomes</option><option value="become">become</option><option value="became">became</option></select> shorter and shift in pronunciation. This shift helps explain why English spelling often seems <select class="pte-select" data-answer="inconsistent"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="inconsistent">inconsistent</option><option value="predictable">predictable</option><option value="identical">identical</option><option value="simple">simple</option></select> compared with how words are pronounced. Linguists still <select class="pte-select" data-answer="actively"><option value="" disabled selected>Select Answer</option><option value="rarely">rarely</option><option value="actively">actively</option><option value="secretly">secretly</option><option value="reluctantly">reluctantly</option></select> debate the precise causes behind this widespread transformation.',
